hadiths[98]{hadithnumber,text}:
"3629","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ related what Jibrīl عليه السلام said to him concerning Ṣalāh. > **""Ṣalāh has been made beloved to you, so take from it as much as you wish.""**"
"3630","Safīnah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ devoted himself to worship before his death and withdrew from women, until he became as though he were a worn-out waterskin."
"3631","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ was once seated with the people around him, and he spoke of what Allah had appointed for every Prophet by way of longing and provision, of his own delight in standing by night, of none praying behind him when he stood, and of the Khums and its disposal after him. > **""Indeed Allah has appointed for every Prophet an object of longing, and my longing lies in standing in prayer by night; when I stand, let none pray behind me. And indeed Allah has appointed for every Prophet a provision, and my provision is this Khums; when I pass away, it belongs to those in authority after me.""**"
"3632","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood in prayer until his feet — or, the narrator was unsure, his shins — swelled, and when he was asked about this despite the forgiveness of his past and future sins, he gave his well-known reply. > **""Shall I not be a grateful slave?""**"
"3633","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray at night until his feet swelled, and that when he was reminded that Allah had forgiven his past and future sins, he answered with his well-known reply. > **""Shall I not be a grateful slave?""**"
"3634","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray until his feet swelled, and that when it was said to him that Allah had already forgiven his past and future sins, he answered with his well-known reply. > **""Shall I not be a grateful slave?""**"
"3635","Al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would stand at night until his feet cracked, and that when he was reminded that Allah had forgiven his past and future sins, he answered with his well-known reply. > **""Shall I not be a grateful slave?""**"
"3636","Abū Juḥayfah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ would pray until his feet swelled, and that when he was reminded that Allah had forgiven his past and future sins, he answered with his well-known reply. > **""Shall I not be a grateful slave?""**"
"3637","Abū Ayyūb رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would use the Miswāk two or three times during the night, and that when he rose to pray at night he would pray four Rakʿahs without speaking or giving any instruction in between, offering the Salām after every two Rakʿahs."
"3638","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would pray sixteen Rakʿahs at night apart from the obligatory prayer."
"3639","ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنه reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had prayed ʿIshāʾ, he would pray four Rakʿahs and then observe Witr with a single Rakʿah, continuing thus until he prayed after his night prayer."
"3640","Ṣafwān ibn al-Muʿaṭṭal al-Sulamī رضي الله عنه reports that while travelling with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ he watched his prayer one night: the Prophet ﷺ prayed the last ʿIshāʾ, then slept; at midnight he awoke, recited the last ten verses of Sūrah Āl ʿImrān, used the Miswāk, performed Wuḍūʾ, and stood to pray two Rakʿahs in which standing, bowing, and prostration were all prolonged beyond distinction. He then slept and repeated the same, continuing as at first until he had prayed eleven Rakʿahs."
"3641","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reports that when it was mentioned to her that some people complete the Qurʾān once or twice in a single night, she remarked that they had recited and yet not truly recited, and she described standing with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ through the night of completion while he recited al-Baqarah, Āl ʿImrān, and al-Nisāʾ, supplicating to Allah and seeking His refuge at every verse of warning, and supplicating to Allah and turning to Him in longing at every verse of glad tidings."
"3642","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would fast continuously until it was said he would never break his fast, and would break his fast continuously until it was said he would never fast, and that he would recite Banū Isrāʾīl and al-Zumar every night."
"3643","Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh رضي الله عنه reports that on the occasion of Ḥudaybiyah they halted at al-Suqyā, where Muʿādh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه asked who would fetch water in their skins; Jābir set out with some young men of the Anṣār to the water at al-Uthāyah, some thirteen miles off, and they filled their skins. After nightfall a man was struggling with his camel toward the trough seeking water, and he proved to be the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Jābir took the halter of his she-camel and made it kneel. The Prophet ﷺ stood and prayed ʿIshāʾ with Jābir at his side, then prayed thirteen Rakʿahs after it."
"3644","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reports that she heard the Prophet ﷺ saying in one Rakʿah of the night prayer the declaration of Divine Oneness. > **""Lā ilāha illā Anta.""**"
"3645","Jasrah bint Dijājah reports that she set out for ʿUmrah and reached al-Rabadhah, where she heard Abū Dharr رضي الله عنه narrating that the Prophet ﷺ stood one night and led the people in prayer, then his Companions remained praying after him; seeing them standing and remaining behind, he withdrew to his lodging, and when he saw the place had emptied he returned and prayed. Abū Dharr stood behind him, and at a gesture with his right hand moved to his right; then Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه came and stood behind them both, and at a gesture with his left hand moved to his left. The three prayed, each for himself, reciting of the Qurʾān whatever he wished, while the Prophet ﷺ stood repeating a single verse of the Qurʾān until he prayed the dawn prayer. In the morning Abū Dharr signalled to ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd to ask him what he had intended by his deed of the night before, but Ibn Masʿūd refused to ask until the Prophet ﷺ spoke of it himself. Abū Dharr then asked him directly, by his father and mother, how he could stand the whole night upon one verse though the whole Qurʾān was with him, saying that had any of them done so they would have been reproached for it. The Prophet ﷺ explained that he had spent the night in supplication, and told of the answer he received and of Abū Dharr's request to convey the glad tidings. Abū Dharr set off a short distance, a stone's throw, when ʿUmar رضي الله عنه warned that the people would rely upon it and abandon worship, so Abū Dharr was called back and returned. The verse in question was the verse of Sūrah al-Māʾidah concerning His servants, His forgiveness, His might, and His wisdom. > **""I was supplicating for my Ummah. I was answered with that which, were many of them to glance upon it, they would abandon Ṣalāh.""**"
"3646","Abū Saʿīd رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ repeated a single verse until morning came."
"3647","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that whenever he came upon the Prophet ﷺ at dawn, he found him dividing Sūrah al-Baqarah between two Rakʿahs of prayer."
"3648","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would divide Sūrah al-Baqarah between two Rakʿahs."
"3649","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was found to show traces of exertion in the morning, and when this was remarked upon, he explained it by his night's recitation. > **""Given what you see of me, I recited last night the seven long Sūrahs.""**"
"3650","Anas رضi الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ would pray in his chamber when some of his Companions came and prayed following his prayer; he would enter the house and come out again repeatedly, praying each time. In the morning they told him they had prayed with him and wished he would prolong his prayer, and he explained his deliberate restraint. > **""I knew of your place, and I did that deliberately.""**"
"3651","Jābir رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ would use the Miswāk two or three times at night: each time he slept and awoke, he would use the Miswāk, perform Wuḍūʾ, and pray two Rakʿahs or one Rakʿah."
"3652","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would recite ten verses from the end of Sūrah Āl ʿImrān every night."
"3653","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها gives a detailed account of the Prophet's ﷺ night: he would pray ʿIshāʾ, then pray in the mosque before returning home, offering salutations every two Rakʿahs and observing Witr with three Rakʿahs in which he sat for Tashahhud after the first two as at the Salām, reciting the Muʿawwidhāt in the Witr. Returning home he would pray two Rakʿahs and sleep; upon waking he would praise Allah for granting him sleep and waking in well-being, raise his head to the sky in reflection, and recite from Sūrah Āl ʿImrān from His words concerning the creation not being in vain until His promise unfailing. He would then perform Wuḍūʾ and pray two long Rakʿahs with extended recitation, bowing, prostration, and abundant supplication, so that ʿĀʾishah would sleep and wake during them; then he would lie down and doze, rise and repeat the same words, and pray two Rakʿahs longer than the first with still more earnest entreaty and seeking of forgiveness, continuing thus until the end of the night. He would then doze briefly until the Muʾadhdhin came, repeat the same words, sit and call for the Miswāk, use it, perform Wuḍūʾ, pray two light Rakʿahs, and go out to Ṣalāh — thirteen Rakʿahs in all. > **""All praise belongs to Allah, who put me to sleep in well-being and awakened me in well-being. Our Lord, You have not created this in vain; glory be to You, so shield us from the punishment of the Fire.""**"
"3654","Ḥudhayfah رضي الله عنه reports that he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was praying and prayed following his prayer behind him without his knowledge. The Prophet ﷺ opened with al-Baqarah, and Ḥudhayfah expected him to bow and yet he carried on; Sinān, one of the narrators, added that he prayed four Rakʿahs with bowing like his standing. When Ḥudhayfah mentioned this to the Prophet ﷺ, he asked why he had not been told, and Ḥudhayfah swore by the One who sent him with the truth that he still felt it in his back even then. The Prophet ﷺ replied that his prayer would have been otherwise had he known. > **""Did you not inform me? Had I known you were behind me, I would have shortened.""**"
"3655","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that he spent the night at the house of his maternal aunt Maymūnah رضي الله عنها. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ rose in alarm, called for water, and performed Wuḍūʾ. He recited from the verse concerning the creation of the heavens and the earth to the end of the Sūrah, then opened al-Baqarah and recited it letter by letter until he completed it. He bowed, prostrated, and sat between the two prostrations with the appointed words of glorification and supplication. He then stood for the second Rakʿah and recited Āl ʿImrān, bowing and prostrating as in the first. He lay down, then rose again in alarm and did as in the first two, reciting letter by letter, until he had prayed eight Rakʿahs, lying down between every two, then observed Witr with three, then prayed the two Rakʿahs of Fajr. > **""Glory be to my Lord, the Most Great. Glory be to my Lord, the Most High. My Lord, forgive me, have mercy upon me, grant me provision, and guide me.""**"
"3656","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had given his father a young she-camel as a gift, but his father found it too small and sent Ibn ʿAbbās back to the Prophet ﷺ to say that they were working people and to ask for an older one in its place. The Prophet ﷺ instructed him to direct it to the Ṣadaqah camels, which he did. He then joined the Prophet ﷺ in the mosque for ʿIshāʾ, after which the Prophet ﷺ asked whether he wished to spend the night at his aunt's house since evening had come, and Ibn ʿAbbās agreed to the Prophet's ﷺ own night there. He went to Maymūnah رضي الله عنها, who gave him supper and spread a cloak folded in four for his bedding, while he resolved to observe what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ did that night. The Prophet ﷺ entered, called out to Maymūnah, asked whether her nephew had come and whether she had given him supper and bedding, and she confirmed it. He inclined to his bed without lying upon it, laying his head upon the bedding nearby, and after a while Ibn ʿAbbās heard him breathing in sleep and supposed he would not rise that night. When a quarter or a third of the night had passed, the Prophet ﷺ rose, went to his Miswāk and water for Wuḍūʾ, and used the Miswāk until the rasping upon his teeth could be heard, reciting the verses concerning the creation of the heavens and the earth; then he put down the Miswāk, went to a waterskin, and loosened its tie. Ibn ʿAbbās wished to rise and pour for him but feared interrupting any part of his practice. After Wuḍūʾ the Prophet ﷺ entered his place of prayer and prayed four Rakʿahs, reciting some fifty verses in each with prolonged bowing and prostration, then returned to his resting place and lay down lightly, breathing in sleep, so that Ibn ʿAbbās supposed he would not rise until morning. When a third or half the night or thereabouts had passed, he rose and did the same, praying four Rakʿahs in like measure, then returned to his bed and breathed in sleep. When a sixth of the night or less remained, he rose, used the Miswāk, performed Wuḍūʾ, and opened with the Fātiḥah followed by al-Aʿlā, then bowed and prostrated; he stood again and recited the Fātiḥah with Qul Hūwa Allāhu Aḥad, then observed Qunūt, bowed, and prostrated; when he finished he sat until dawn broke, then called to Ibn ʿAbbās, who answered, and commanded him to rise. > **""Rise, for by Allah, you were not asleep.""**"
"3657","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that his father sent him to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with a need, and he found him seated with his Companions in the mosque and could not address him. After praying Maghrib the Prophet ﷺ stood offering voluntary Rakʿahs until all who remained in the mosque had departed, then went to his house with Ibn ʿAbbās following him. Hearing his footsteps, the Prophet ﷺ asked who it was, and upon learning it was Ibn ʿAbbās, the cousin of the Messenger of Allah, he welcomed him warmly. > **""Who is this? Welcome to the cousin of the Messenger of Allah.""**"
"3658","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would keep the night alive with eight Rakʿahs whose bowing and prostration were like their recitation, offering the Salām between every two Rakʿahs."
"3659","Nāfiʿ ibn Khālid al-Khuzāʿī reports from his father رضي الله عنه that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed while the people were watching, he would pray a short prayer complete in its bowing and prostration."
"3660","Muʿāwiyah ibn al-Ḥakam رضي الله عنه reports, in the manner of the narration of Mālik concerning the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, eleven Rakʿahs and his lying upon his right side."
"3661","Al-Ḥajjāj ibn Ghaziyah رضي الله عنه, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reports that one of them supposes that standing in prayer through the night until morning constitutes Tahajjud, whereas Tahajjud is only prayer after a sleep, then prayer after a sleep — and such was the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."
"3662","Al-Ḥajjāj ibn ʿAmr al-Māzinī رضي الله عنه reports that one of them supposes that standing in prayer until morning constitutes Tahajjud, whereas Tahajjud is prayer after a sleep, then prayer after a sleep — and such was the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who would use the Miswāk before his Tahajjud."
"3663","ʿUthmān رضي الله عنه reports that he performed Wuḍūʾ three times over and then related that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ performed Wuḍūʾ like his own, after which he described the reward of one who performs Wuḍūʾ likewise and prays two Rakʿahs entertaining nothing but good within them. > **""Whoever performs Wuḍūʾ like this Wuḍūʾ of mine, then prays two Rakʿahs in which he speaks to himself of nothing but good, his past sins are forgiven.""**"
"3664","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described the reward of one who performs Wuḍūʾ well and prays four Rakʿahs without lapsing in them. > **""Whoever performs Wuḍūʾ and perfects it, then prays four Rakʿahs in which he does not lapse, he is forgiven.""**"
"3665","Rabīʿah ibn Qays reports that he heard ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir رضي الله عنه say that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ describe the expiation of one who performs Wuḍūʾ well and prays without forgetfulness or heedlessness. > **""Whoever performs Wuḍūʾ and perfects his Wuḍūʾ, then prays without forgetfulness or heedlessness, what was upon him of evil deeds is expiated.""**"
"3666","Yūsuf ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Salām reports that he kept company with Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه to learn from him, and that when death approached him, Abū al-Dardāʾ asked that the people be informed. The house and its surroundings filled. He asked to be brought out and seated, then addressed the people with what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning two complete Rakʿahs after perfect Wuḍūʾ, adding his own warning against turning about in Ṣalāh and against allowing it in the obligatory prayers even if overcome in the voluntary. > **""Whoever performs Wuḍūʾ and perfects it, then prays two Rakʿahs and completes them, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, grants him whatever he asks, sooner or later. Beware of turning about in Ṣalāh, for there is no prayer for one who turns about; if you are overcome in the voluntary, then let it not be in the obligatory.""**"
"3667","Yūsuf ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Salām reports that he visited Abū al-Dardāʾ رضي الله عنه in the illness in which he died. Abū al-Dardāʾ asked what had brought him to that land, and Yūsuf replied that it was only the bond between Abū al-Dardāʾ and his father ʿAbdullāh ibn Salām رضي الله عنه. Abū al-Dardāʾ declared it an evil hour for falsehood and then related what he had heard from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning Wuḍūʾ, prayer of two or four Rakʿahs with excellence in bowing and humility — or, in another wording, obligatory or voluntary prayer with excellence in bowing and prostration — followed by seeking Allah's forgiveness. > **""Whoever performs Wuḍūʾ and perfects it, then stands and prays two or four Rakʿahs, performing well in them the bowing and humility, then seeks Allah's forgiveness, he is forgiven.""**"
"3668","ʿUthmān ibn Ḥunayf رضي الله عنه reports that a man repeatedly brought a need to ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān رضي الله عنه without receiving attention, and upon complaining of this to ʿUthmān ibn Ḥunayf, he was instructed to perform Wuḍūʾ, pray two Rakʿahs in the mosque, supplicate through the Prophet ﷺ, the Prophet of Mercy, mentioning his need, and then come at the time of going to ʿUthmān. The man did so; the doorkeeper took him by the hand to ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, who seated him with himself upon his carpet, fulfilled his need, and reproached him for his delay while bidding him return with every need. When the man thanked ʿUthmān ibn Ḥunayf and supposed he had interceded for him, ʿUthmān ibn Ḥunayf swore he had not spoken to him, but recalled witnessing a blind man come to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ complaining of his lost sight. The Prophet ﷺ first offered him patience, but when the man explained he had no guide and found it grievous, he instructed him to perform Wuḍūʾ, pray two Rakʿahs, and supplicate with those words. ʿUthmān ibn Ḥunayf swore that they had not dispersed nor long conversed before the man returned as though never afflicted. > **""O Allah, I ask You and turn to You through our Prophet Muḥammad ﷺ, the Prophet of Mercy. O Muḥammad, I turn through you to my Lord, that He may fulfil my need for me. Would you be patient? Go to the place of Wuḍūʾ, perform Wuḍūʾ, then pray two Rakʿahs, then supplicate with these words.""**"
"3669","Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ described seeking Istikhārah of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, as part of the felicity of the son of Adam, and, in the fuller wording, described contentment with His decree as part of felicity and abandoning Istikhārah with resentment after the decree as part of wretchedness. > **""Part of the felicity of the son of Adam is his seeking Istikhārah of Allah, Mighty and Majestic; part of the felicity of a man is his seeking Istikhārah of his Lord and his contentment with what He decrees, and part of the wretchedness of a man is his abandoning Istikhārah and his resentment after the decree.""**"
"3670","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that seeking guidance, taking counsel, and living with moderation preserve from loss, regret, and need. > **""He who seeks Istikhārah is not disappointed, he who takes counsel does not regret, and he who lives with moderation is not reduced to need.""**"
"3671","Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed one concealing a proposal of marriage to perform Wuḍūʾ well, pray whatever Allah has decreed for him, praise and glorify his Lord, and then supplicate by Allah's power and knowledge, naming the woman, for her decree if she holds good for his worldly life and Hereafter. > **""Conceal the proposal, then perform Wuḍūʾ and perfect it, then pray whatever Allah has decreed for you, then praise your Lord and glorify Him, then say: O Allah, You decree and I do not decree, You know and I do not know, and You are the Knower of the unseen; if You see in so-and-so — naming her by name — good for me in my worldly life and my Hereafter, then decree her for me.""**"
"3672","Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī رضي الله عنه, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, reports the same instruction given to him: to conceal the proposal, perfect Wuḍūʾ, pray whatever Allah has decreed, praise and glorify his Lord, and supplicate by Allah's power and knowledge, naming the woman, for her decree if she holds good for his religion, worldly life, and Hereafter. > **""Conceal the proposal, then perform Wuḍūʾ and perfect it, then pray whatever Allah has decreed for you, then praise your Lord and glorify Him, then say: O Allah, You decree and I do not decree, You know and I do not know, and You are the Knower of the unseen; if You see in so-and-so — naming her by name — good for me in my religion, my worldly life, and my Hereafter, then decree her for me.""**"
"3673","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reports that when the Prophet ﷺ sought Istikhārah in a matter he intended to undertake, he would supplicate by Allah's knowledge and power, asking of His bounty, confessing his own want of power and knowledge against Allah's perfect power, knowledge of the unseen, and bounty; asking for choice in well-being, ease, and blessing if the matter held good for his religion, livelihood, and pursuit of good, and for the decree of good wherever it lay otherwise — and in the wording of al-Muʿjam al-Ṣaghīr, for the averting of evil wherever it lay and contentment with His decree — after which he would resolve. > **""O Allah, I seek Your choice by Your knowledge, I seek Your power by Your power, and I ask You of Your bounty; for You decree and I do not decree, You know and I do not know, and You are the Knower of the unseen. O Allah, if this is good for me in my religion, good for me in my livelihood, and good for me in what I seek of good thereby, then choose it for me in well-being, ease it for me, then bless me in it; and if other than that is better for me, then decree for me the good wherever it is, avert evil from me wherever it is, and make me content with Your decree.""**"
"3674","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reports, in another narration, that when the Prophet ﷺ sought Istikhārah in a matter he intended to undertake, he spoke to the same effect, with the wording of choosing in well-being and easing, and with the addition of asking of Allah's bounty and mercy which none possesses but Him, and of granting success and ease therein. > **""Choose for me in well-being and ease it for me. I ask You of Your bounty and Your mercy, for they are in Your Hand and none possesses them but You; grant me success in it and ease it for me.""**"
"3675","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ taught them Istikhārah, instructing each of them to supplicate by Allah's knowledge and power, asking of His bounty, confessing his own want of power and knowledge, naming the matter by name, and asking for its decree and blessing if it held good for his religion, livelihood, outcome, and all affairs, and otherwise for the decree of good wherever it lay and contentment with it. > **""O Allah, I seek Your choice by Your knowledge, I seek Your power by Your power, and I ask You of Your bounty; for You decree and I do not decree, You know and I do not know, and You are the Knower of the unseen. If such-and-such — naming the matter by name — is good for me in my religion, my livelihood, the outcome of my affair, and all my affairs, then decree it for me and bless me in it; and if other than that is better for me, then decree for me the good wherever it is and make me content with it.""**"
"3676","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما report that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would teach them Istikhārah as he would teach them a Sūrah of the Qurʾān, with its supplication by His knowledge and power, the confession of want of power and knowledge, and the petition that whatever He decrees be made to end in good. > **""O Allah, I seek Your choice by Your knowledge and I seek Your power by Your power; for You decree and I do not decree, You know and I do not know, and You are the Knower of the unseen. O Allah, whatever decree You decree for me, make its outcome toward good.""**"
"3677","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reports that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instruct that whoever among them intends a matter should supplicate by Allah's knowledge and power, asking of His bounty, confessing his own want of power and knowledge, naming the matter, asking for it if it holds good for his religion, livelihood, and outcome, and otherwise asking for its aversion, self-aversion from it, and the decree of good wherever it lies, for there is no power nor strength except through Allah. > **""When one of you intends a matter, let him say: O Allah, I seek Your choice by Your knowledge, I seek Your power by Your power, and I ask You of Your bounty; for You decree and I do not decree, You know and I do not know, and You are the Knower of the unseen. O Allah, if such-and-such — concerning what he intends — is good for me in my religion, my livelihood, and the outcome of my affair, then let it be; otherwise avert it from me and avert me from it, then decree for me the good wherever it is. There is no power nor strength except through Allah.""**"
"3678","ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that al-ʿAbbās رضي الله عنه came to the Prophet ﷺ at an hour he was unaccustomed to visit. When it was announced that his uncle stood at the door, the Prophet ﷺ ordered him admitted, saying he must have come for some matter. Upon entering, and being asked what had brought him at that unusual hour, al-ʿAbbās explained that the remembrance of Jāhiliyyah and its ignorance had made the world straiten him despite its vastness, and that he had concluded none could relieve him but Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and then the Prophet ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ praised Allah for casting this into his heart, expressed his wish that Abū Ṭālib had taken his share though Allah does as He wills, and offered him a gift. He then instructed him to perfect his purification at an hour of prayer outside the times after ʿAṣr and after sunrise, to stand before Allah, recite the Fātiḥah with a Sūrah — or, if he wished, from the beginning of the Mufaṣṣal — and upon finishing the Sūrah to say the glorification fifteen times, ten times in bowing, and ten times upon raising his head. > **""Shall I inform you? Shall I give to you? Shall I grant to you? When there comes an hour in which you may pray — neither after ʿAṣr nor after sunrise, but between them — then perfect your purification, stand before Allah, Mighty and Majestic, recite the Fātiḥah of the Book with a Sūrah, then say: Glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, there is no god but Allah, and Allah is Most Great, fifteen times; when you bow, say it ten times; when you raise your head, say it ten times.""**"
"3679","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed him as a youth with an offer of a gift, and when Ibn ʿAbbās answered with his father and mother as ransom, supposing a portion of wealth would be assigned to him, the Prophet ﷺ instead instructed him in four Rakʿahs to be prayed daily, or weekly, or monthly, or yearly, or once in a lifetime: to magnify Allah, recite the Mother of the Qurʾān with a Sūrah, then say the glorification fifteen times standing, ten times in bowing, ten times upon rising, ten times in prostration, ten times upon rising, and likewise through all of the prayer; and when finished, to supplicate after the Tashahhud before the Salām with the comprehensive supplication for the success of the guided, the deeds of the certain, the counsel of the repentant, the resolve of the patient, the earnestness of the God-fearing, the seeking of the desirous, the worship of the Godwary, and the discernment of the learned, with fear that restrains from disobedience, obedience earning His pleasure, sincere repentance from His fear, sincere counsel from His love, and trust in Him with good opinion of Him — glory to the Creator of the Fire — with the promise that all sins, small and great, old and new, secret and open, deliberate and mistaken, would be forgiven. > **""O youth, shall I grant to you? Shall I bestow upon you? Shall I give to you? Pray four Rakʿahs every day; if you cannot, then every Friday; if you cannot, then every month; if you cannot, then every year; if you cannot, then once in your lifetime. Magnify Allah, recite the Mother of the Qurʾān with a Sūrah, then say: Glory be to Allah, praise belongs to Allah, there is no god but Allah, and Allah is Most Great, fifteen times; in bowing say it ten times, upon rising ten times, in prostration ten times, upon rising ten times, and do likewise through all of your prayer. When you finish, say after the Tashahhud and before the Salām: O Allah, I ask You for the success of the people of guidance, the deeds of the people of certainty, the counsel of the people of repentance, the resolve of the people of patience, the earnestness of the people of awe, the seeking of the people of longing, the worship of the people of Godwariness, and the discernment of the people of knowledge, that I may fear You. O Allah, I ask You for a fear that restrains me from Your disobedience, that I may act in Your obedience with deeds earning Your pleasure, counsel You in repentance from fear of You, make counsel sincere for You from love of You, and trust in You in all affairs with good opinion of You. Glory be to the Creator of the Fire. If you do that, O Ibn ʿAbbās, Allah forgives your sins, small and great, old and new, secret and open, deliberate and mistaken.""**"
"3680","Ibn ʿAbbās رضي الله عنهما reports, through Abū al-Jawzāʾ, that Ibn ʿAbbās addressed him with the offer of a gift, and upon his acceptance related that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ speak of four Rakʿahs to the same effect in abridged form, with the promise of forgiveness attached. > **""Whoever prays four Rakʿahs, he is forgiven every sin, small and great, old and new, past or to come.""**"
"3681","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf رضي الله عنه reports that four or five of the Companions never parted from the Prophet ﷺ or his door. One night the Prophet ﷺ went out and Ibn ʿAwf followed him into a walled garden among the gardens of the market town, where the Prophet ﷺ prayed and prolonged his prostration. Fearing Allah had taken the soul of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and that he would never see him again, Ibn ʿAwf grieved and wept. The Prophet ﷺ raised his head, called him, and asked what ailed him; when Ibn ʿAwf explained his fear at the length of the prostration, the Prophet ﷺ told him it was a prostration of gratitude to his Lord for what He had bestowed upon his Ummah, conveying His word concerning ten good deeds recorded for each invocation of blessing upon him. > **""I performed this prostration in gratitude to my Lord for what He has bestowed upon me concerning my Ummah: He said, whoever among them invokes one blessing upon you, ten good deeds are recorded for him.""**"
"3682","Anas ibn Mālik رضي الله عنه reports that whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ halted at a place, he would not depart from it until he had bidden it farewell with two Rakʿahs."
"3683","Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd رضي الله عنه reports that whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ halted at a place on a journey or entered his house, he would not sit until he had prayed two Rakʿahs."
"3684","ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه reports that a man came to the Prophet ﷺ and told him he intended to set out for al-Baḥrayn in trade, whereupon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ instructed him to pray before departing. > **""Pray two Rakʿahs.""**"
"3685","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه reports that whenever the Messenger of Allah ﷺ arrived from a journey, he would pray two Rakʿahs."
"3686","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ instructed that two Rakʿahs upon entering and two upon leaving the house guard both passages from evil. > **""When you enter your house, pray two Rakʿahs; they will prevent you from the evil entrance. When you leave your house, pray two Rakʿahs; they will prevent you from the evil exit.""**"
"3687","Anas رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ said that when the son of Adam prostrates, Satan declares his own contrasting lot: Paradise for the obedient prostration of Adam's son, and the Fire for his own refusal — or words to that effect. > **""The son of Adam was commanded to prostrate, so he prostrated, and Paradise is his; I was commanded to prostrate, so I refused, and the Fire is mine.""**"
"3688","Abū Isḥāq reports that ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said that when Satan sees the son of Adam prostrating, he cries out in woe upon himself and upon Satan: Allah commanded the son of Adam to prostrate with Paradise as his reward and he obeyed, while He commanded Satan to prostrate and he disobeyed, so the Fire is his."
"3689","Makhramah ibn Nawfal رضي الله عنه reports that when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ openly proclaimed Islam, all the people of Makkah embraced it, before Ṣalāh was made obligatory — such that when he recited a verse of prostration they would all prostrate, some unable to prostrate for the press of the crowd — until the chiefs of Quraysh, al-Walīd ibn al-Mughīrah, Abū Jahl ibn Hishām, and others who had been at al-Ṭāʾif upon their lands, arrived and said they were abandoning the religion of their forefathers, whereupon they disbelieved."
"3690","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reports that he saw in a dream that he was writing Ṣād, and that when he reached its verse of prostration, the inkwell, the pen, and all present turned and prostrated. When he related it to the Prophet ﷺ, the Prophet ﷺ thereafter never ceased to prostrate at it."
"3691","Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī رضي الله عنه reports that he saw in his sleep as though he were beneath a tree reciting Ṣād, and that when it reached the prostration it prostrated, supplicating in its prostration for forgiveness, the removal of burden, the renewal of gratitude, and acceptance as was accepted from His servant Dāwūd. The next morning he informed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who asked whether he himself had prostrated; when he said he had not, the Prophet ﷺ told him he was more entitled to prostration than the tree, then recited Sūrah Ṣād himself, and upon reaching the prostration said in his prostration what the tree had said in its prostration. > **""Did you prostrate? You are more entitled to prostration than the tree. O Allah, forgive me through it; O Allah, remove a burden from me through it, renew gratitude in me through it, and accept it from me as You accepted the prostration of Your servant Dāwūd.""**"
"3692","Masrūq reports that ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه said that the passage was the repentance of a Prophet mentioned therein, and for that reason he would not prostrate in it — meaning Ṣād."
"3693","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ prostrated in Ṣād."
"3694","ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān رضي الله عنه is reported to have prostrated in Ṣād."
"3695","ʿAlī رضي الله عنه reports that the binding prostrations are four: the one in Alif Lām Mīm, the Revelation, al-Sajdah; the one in Ḥā Mīm, al-Sajdah; the one in al-Najm; and the one in Read in the Name of your Lord."
"3696","ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aswad report that ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه would prostrate at the first verse of Ḥā Mīm, the Revelation from the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate."
"3697","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that Sūrah al-Najm was being written down in the presence of the Prophet ﷺ, and that when he reached the prostration he prostrated, they prostrated with him, and the inkwell and the pen prostrated."
"3698","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reports that he prayed three times behind the Prophet ﷺ when he recited the prostration in the obligatory prayer."
"3699","Abū Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reports that the Prophet ﷺ and the Muslims prostrated at al-Najm, except for two men of Quraysh who abstained thereby seeking renown."
"3700","ʿĀʾishah رضي الله عنها reports that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited al-Najm, and that when he reached the prostration he prostrated."
"3701","ʿAmr al-Jinnī رضي الله عنه reports that he was with the Prophet ﷺ when he recited Sūrah al-Najm and prostrated, and that he prostrated along with him."
"3702","Ibn ʿUmar رضي الله عنهما reports that the Prophet ﷺ recited al-Najm at Makkah and prostrated, and the people prostrated with him, until a man would lift something of the earth to his forehead and prostrate upon it, and one man would prostrate upon another."
"3703","Ibn Masʿūd رضي الله عنه is reported to have prostrated at al-Najm whenever he recited it before the people, and to have bowed and prostrated with it whenever he recited it in Ṣalāh."
"3704","On the authority of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه, from the Prophet ﷺ, it is reported that the narrator saw him prostrate in Surah Idha al-Sama unshaqqat, and this was observed ten times."
"3705","Safwan ibn Assal رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prostrated in Surah Idha al-Sama unshaqqat."
"3706","Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه prayed the morning prayer and recited Surah Idha al-Sama unshaqqat therein, and he prostrated in it."
"3707","Al-Aswad ibn Yazid said that he saw Abdullah رضي الله عنه and Umar رضي الله عنه — or, with doubt from the narrator, one of the two — performing the prostration in Surah Idha al-Sama unshaqqat."
"3708","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه used to prostrate in Surah al-Najm and in Surah Iqra Bismi Rabbik alladhi Khalaq."
"3709","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه gave a ruling concerning one who recites Surah al-Araf, Surah al-Najm, and Surah Iqra Bismi Rabbik alladhi Khalaq, explaining the sufficiency of bowing and the manner of prostrating and then continuing. > **""Whoever recites al-Araf, al-Najm, and Iqra Bismi Rabbik alladhi Khalaq, then if he wishes he may bow with it and it will suffice him, and if he wishes he may prostrate, then stand, recite the surah, and prostrate.""**"
"3710","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه gave a ruling concerning one who recites Surah al-Araf, al-Najm, Iqra Bismi Rabbik, Idha al-Sama unshaqqat, or Bani Israil, explaining when bowing at the end suffices and what one who prostrates should then recite. > **""Whoever recites Surah al-Araf, or al-Najm, or Iqra Bismi Rabbik, or Idha al-Sama unshaqqat, or Bani Israil, then if he wishes to bow at the end of them, let him bow; the bowing suffices him for the prostration. And if he prostrates, let him add another surah to it.""**"
"3711","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه gave a ruling concerning the prostration that occurs at the end of a surah, allowing either bowing or prostrating. > **""When the prostration is at the end of the surah, then bow if you wish, or prostrate, for indeed the prostration is with the bowing.""**"
"3712","Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه likewise gave a ruling concerning the prostration at the end of a surah and its conjunction with the bowing. > **""When the prostration is at the end of the surah, then bow if you wish, or prostrate, for indeed the prostration is with the bowing.""**"
"3713","Ata ibn al-Saib said that they used to recite before Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami while he was walking, and that when they passed a verse of prostration, he would pronounce the takbir and they would pronounce it with him, and he would prostrate and they would prostrate with him. He would then raise his head with takbir and greet them with peace, and they would return the greeting to him. Abu Abd al-Rahman held that Abdullah رضي الله عنه used to do the same with them."
"3714","Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out and turned toward his upper chamber, then entered it and faced the qiblah and fell into prostration. He prolonged the prostration until Abd al-Rahman thought that Allah had taken his soul in it, so he drew near to him, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ raised his head and asked who was there and what his matter was. Abd al-Rahman replied that he had feared the soul of the Messenger ﷺ had been taken in that prostration. The Prophet ﷺ then explained the glad tidings brought by Jibril عليه السلام concerning divine mercy and peace for those who invoke salah and salam upon him, and the reason for his prostration in gratitude to Allah. > **""Jibril came to me and gave me glad tidings, saying: Indeed Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says: Whoever sends salah upon you, I shall send salah upon him, and whoever sends salam upon you, I shall send salam upon him. So I prostrated to Allah in gratitude.""**"
"3715","Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was absent from them and did not come out until they thought he would not come out. When he did come out, he fell into a prostration so long that they thought his soul had been taken in it. When he raised his head, he described how his Lord had consulted him concerning his ummah and what He would do with them, how he had twice entrusted the matter to his Lord as His creation and servants, and how his Lord had then reassured him concerning his ummah. The compiler noted that the full narration will come, God willing, in the signs of prophethood or in the virtues of the ummah. > **""Indeed my Lord, Mighty and Majestic, consulted me concerning my ummah, asking what He should do with them. I said: Whatever You will, my Lord; they are Your creation and Your servants. He consulted me a second time and I said the same to Him. He said: I will not grieve you concerning your ummah, O Muhammad.""**"
"3716","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported in marfu form from the Prophet ﷺ an account of a man who passed by the skull of a human being and reflected within himself, then fell prostrate, whereupon a voice addressed him distinguishing his station from that of his Lord. > **""A man passed by the skull of a human being and spoke to himself, then fell prostrate. It was said to him: Raise your head, for you are you and I am I.""**"
"3717","Umar ibn al-Khattab رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out and found no one following him, so Umar became alarmed and came with a water-skin of leather, and found the Prophet ﷺ prostrating in his upper chamber. Umar withdrew behind him until the Prophet ﷺ raised his head, commended him for withdrawing upon finding him in prostration, and informed him of what Jibril عليه السلام had brought concerning the tenfold reward for sending salah upon him. > **""You did well, O Umar, when you found me prostrating and withdrew from me. Indeed Jibril came to me and said: Whoever from your ummah sends one salah upon you, Allah will send ten upon him and raise him ten degrees thereby.""**"
"3718","Abu Qatadah رضي الله عنه reported that Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه went out seeking the Messenger of Allah ﷺ but did not find him, so he searched for him in his houses and did not find him, then followed street after street until he was directed to him at Mount Thawr. He ascended it, looked right and left, and saw him in the cave toward which people had made a path to the Mosque of al-Fath. Muadh found him prostrating, and he did not raise his head until Muadh thought ill of the matter and supposed his soul had been taken. When the Prophet ﷺ raised his head, Muadh expressed his fear, and the Prophet ﷺ explained the two visits of Jibril عليه السلام at that place, the greeting and question conveyed from Allah concerning his ummah, and his prostration because of the excellence of prostration as a means of nearness. > **""Jibril came to me in this place and said: Indeed Allah, Blessed and Exalted, conveys salam to you and says to you: What do you wish that I do with your ummah? I said: Allah knows best. He went away, then came back to me and said: He says to you: I will not displease you concerning your ummah. So I prostrated, and the most excellent thing by which nearness is sought to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is prostration.""**"
"3719","Muadh ibn Jabal رضي الله عنه reported that he came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while he was standing in prayer, and he remained standing until morning, then fell into a prostration so long that Muadh thought his soul had been taken in it. The Prophet ﷺ asked him whether he knew the reason for it, and when Muadh entrusted knowledge to Allah and His Messenger, the Prophet ﷺ repeated the question three or four times, then described the prayer his Lord had decreed for him, the repeated questioning of his Lord concerning his ummah, and his prostration to his Grateful Lord who loves the grateful. > **""I prayed what my Lord had decreed for me, and my Lord came to me and said to me at the end of it: What shall I do with your ummah? I said: My Lord, You know best. He repeated it to me three or four times, then said to me at the end: What shall I do with your ummah? I said: You know best, my Lord. He said: I will not grieve you concerning your ummah. So I prostrated to my Lord, and my Lord is Grateful and loves the grateful.""**"
"3720","Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr رضي الله عنه reported that he came to visit the Messenger of Allah ﷺ while revelation was descending upon him. When the state lifted from him, he asked Aishah رضي الله عنها to hand him his cloak, then went out and entered the mosque, where only a small group was present. He sat with them until the speaker finished his exhortation, then recited Surah Tanzil al-Sajdah and prolonged the prostration until one could have travelled two miles in its duration. The people heard of his prostration and gathered until the mosque could not contain them, and Aishah sent word to her family to attend upon witnessing something unprecedented. When he raised his head, Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه asked about the length of the prostration, and the Prophet ﷺ explained the gift granted to him concerning his ummah. Abu Bakr asked him to seek increase for them, and he did so twice or thrice, whereupon Umar رضي الله عنه expressed his joy that the ummah had been granted to him. The compiler noted that it has other chains to come in the chapter of resurrection, God willing. > **""I prostrated to my Lord in gratitude for what He has granted me concerning my ummah: seventy thousand entering Paradise without reckoning.""**"
"3721","Jarir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ announced that nothing remained of the idols of Jahiliyyah except the house of Dhu al-Khalasah, and asked who would volunteer for Allah and His Messenger. Jarir volunteered, setting out with seven hundred men, all from Ahmas, and they surprised the people with the forelocks of their horses, killed, burned the house, and wrote to the Messenger ﷺ with glad tidings that nothing of it remained but something like a mangy camel. Upon this news the Messenger of Allah ﷺ fell prostrate and supplicated for Ahmas. The compiler noted that it is in the Sahih in similar form with an abridged mention of the prostration. > **""Nothing remains of the tyrants of Jahiliyyah except the house of Dhu al-Khalasah, so who will volunteer for Allah and His Messenger? O Allah, bless Ahmas in its horses and its men.""**"
"3722","Abu Musa رضي الله عنه reported that they were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he saw him perform the prostration of gratitude, and the Prophet ﷺ explained its purpose. > **""I prostrated in gratitude.""**"
"3723","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ passed by a man afflicted with a chronic ailment, so he dismounted and prostrated. Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه then passed by him and likewise dismounted and prostrated, and Umar رضي الله عنه then passed by him and likewise dismounted and prostrated."
"3724","Jabir ibn Abdullah رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ, when he saw a man of altered creation, would prostrate, and when he saw a monkey he would prostrate, and when he rose from sleep he would prostrate."
"3725","Urfajah رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ saw a man afflicted with disability and prostrated, and that Abu Bakr رضي الله عنه came to him after a victory and prostrated, and that Umar رضي الله عنه came to him after a victory and prostrated."
"3726","Asma bint Abi Bakr al-Siddiq رضي الله عنها reported that when Ibn al-Zubayr رضي الله عنهما was killed, she had in her keeping something that the Prophet ﷺ had given in a small basket. She ordered that it be sought, and when it was found, she fell prostrate."
